"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nA work called one of the world's top three mystery novels, I think it's a timeless masterpiece.  \n\nThe story begins when eight men and women who seem to have no connection to each other are invited to a luxurious mansion on 'Indian Island', a remote island off the Devon coast, by a mysterious figure named 'U. N. Owen'.  \nUpon arriving at the island, they learn that the host who invited them is absent, and only a servant couple who had arrived first welcomes them.  \nThus, eight guests and two servants, a total of ten people, are isolated on the island.  \n\nAfter dinner, a mysterious voice suddenly flows from a gramophone, exposing one by one the 'murder' charges of all ten people on the island, who had cleverly evaded legal judgment in the past, along with their names.  \n\nAs they fall into shock and confusion, people start dying one by one, exactly like the lyrics of the nursery rhyme .  \n\nIt's like a dark fable asking the meaning of justice.  "